Preserve the CSS style resolver across memory-pressure purges (default), so the
aggressive low-threshold purge stops thrashing scroll on heavy pages.

releaseCriticalMemory() purges decoded images, the back/forward cache, URL/disk
cache and JS code to bound the process footprint -- AND calls
Document::styleScope().releaseMemory(), which clears the style resolver
(clearResolver + shared shadow-tree resolvers + match cache). The resolver frees
little memory, but rebuilding it costs a full pass over every author stylesheet
(Style::Resolver::appendAuthorStyleSheets, RuleSetBuilder, RuleFeatureSet,
createOrFindSharedShadowTreeResolver).

On this device the memory-pressure base threshold must be kept low
(WEBKIT_MEMORY_BASE_THRESHOLD_MB=700) because a reddit feed grows ~1.5 GB of
mostly-off-RSS memory (decoded images -> GPU textures + caches) that hard-OOMs the
phone, and WebKit's purge watches RSS, not graphics memory. But at that threshold
the critical purge fires on most 3 s polls, and on reddit (many stylesheets +
shadow DOM) the resolver rebuild then runs on every style update DURING scroll --
device-profiled (gdb stack sampling) as ~40% of main-thread samples in
appendAuthorStyleSheets / RuleSetBuilder, pinning the main thread ~88% and starving
tile recording + input (so newly-exposed tiles paint late). The checkerboard hides
tile eviction but does nothing for this.

Skipping just the styleScope purge keeps every footprint-bounding purge intact (so
the OOM protection at base=700 still holds) while removing the rebuild thrash.
Default = preserve; WEBKIT_PURGE_STYLE_ON_MEMORY_PRESSURE=1 restores upstream.

